A bubbles model under a loop that reads neither its index nor its element is
still one per copy, keyed by the index.

The copy key is the loops' indices (lower.CopyKey), which gives a loop binding
only its element an index named __copy0. The walks that find a copy write that
key as text, so a head declaring only what its body names declared nothing and
the key was undefined. The loop also kept the single-variable kind
passIterKind gave it, so a head that did declare the key bound the element to
it. The loop's index is now marked read (ir.For.KeyRead) and its kind derived
again once the index is added. The input has no `#id`: passCopyKeys would give
the loop its index during lowering, before passIterKind, and the IR would name
it.

deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `for _, __copy0 := range` -- __copy0 is the index, not the element
-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
}

var text = ""

ui.window {
    for var i = [1, 2] {
        ui.input(:value=text)
    }
}
-- out/go/bubbletea/model.go --
